; body-vitals-live.fk — the body watches itself and says how it is.
;
; One kernel, no forks, no files. Every second it reads the roster — one shared
; page, no frame opened — folds each surface's stamp into a watch, and gives the
; result back into the membrane as the `vitals` frame.
;
; It names no organ. Every surface teaches its own rhythm, and the only reading
; that asks for attention is a surface that moved, taught a cadence, and then
; departed from ITSELF. A slow publisher is never called sick; a surface seen to
; move fewer than twice has taught no rhythm and nothing is claimed about it.
;
; The signal is a finding, not a mirror: one row carries the whole reading, and
; after it comes one row per surface asking for attention. A body of a hundred
; and fifty healthy surfaces signals in a single row.
;
; This publisher registers like any other, so the watch sees itself and holds
; its own rhythm to the same standard — a vitals organ that stops is caught by
; the next watch that reads the roster.
;
; preludes: form/form-stdlib/hearth.bml form/form-stdlib/form-glass-vitals.bml

(fgv-loop (hearth-telemetry-root) (list))
